\section*{\$d\$-to-\$1\$ Games Conjecture}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 105; 2026-09-23), the asserted result is that exact-two projection games are NP-hard to distinguish between fully satisfiable instances and arbitrarily small fixed value, with a fixed alphabet.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 105; 2026-09-23), matrix slices organize a tree of partial function tables. Joint pointwise evaluations retain perfect completeness, while hidden-child sampling and posterior control decode inconsistent answers without increasing the two-element projection fibers.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Perfect-completeness hardness is established for projections with exactly two preimages. The statement here includes every integer fiber size $d\ge2$, and no theorem or reduction for all those sizes is supplied.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 105; 2026-09-23), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: exact-two projection games are NP-hard to distinguish between fully satisfiable instances and arbitrarily small fixed value, with a fixed alphabet.

This coverage does not establish a formal proof of the complete problem as stated here.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{PerfectCompleteness.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.PerfectCompleteness.Theorem11.exists\_reduction}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item OpenAI, \emph{Perfect completeness for 2-to-1 games}, 2026-09-23; citation key \texttt{OAI:Perfect-completeness-for-2-to-1-games-September-23-2026}. \href{https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/preprints/Perfect-completeness-for-2-to-1-games-September-23-2026/paper.pdf}{PDF (GitHub)}; \href{https://github.com/openai/math/raw/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/preprints/Perfect-completeness-for-2-to-1-games-September-23-2026/paper.pdf}{PDF (direct)}; \href{https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/preprints/Perfect-completeness-for-2-to-1-games-September-23-2026/README.md}{Manuscript page and citation}; \href{https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Perfect-completeness-for-2-to-1-games-September-23-2026/paper.pdf}{Latest version}.
\item Family 105: \href{https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/lean/docs/105.md}{Lean scope}.
\item \href{https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/lean/ComparatorChallenges/PerfectCompleteness.json}{Comparator PerfectCompleteness.json}.
\item \href{https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/lean/ComparatorChallenges/PerfectCompleteness.lean}{Comparator PerfectCompleteness.lean}.
\item \href{https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/CONTENTS.md}{OpenAI catalogue entry}.
\end{itemize}
